as hurt right and so they  associated Gulf with pain yeah that was  that's you know still is one of the  tougher things that they're both excited  I'm playing again but also you okay dad  you know that kind of thing because they  they remember those times when dad  couldn't get off the couch dad was  laying there and there's excited that  I'm playing and I'm happy I'm doing  something I love love to do and there  see not in me but they also stand again  they're there they're worried for for  dad  cuz they don't want their I don't want  that hurt again are you more when you  look at yourself as a parent are you  more like your mom was with you or like  your dad was with you oh definitely like  my dad definitely my my mom's tough very  very tough yeah  very vocal my dad was more cerebral and  like the plant seeds that that didn't  that wouldn't germinate for years but  there was like oh yeah that's what I'd  like to do and don't need to yell at  kids all the time I'm not like that my  mom was like that very strict more  afraid and and you know even though my  dad was a special horses I was never  really afraid of dad I talked to you  maybe probably 10 years ago maybe it was  a little bit less than that just about  your dad and what you think he would  think of who you've become as a man as a  father what do you think your dad would  think right now Pop's would be proud I  mean he says yeah you made your share  mistakes we all do and  you bounce-back learn from and you know  you always wanted me to think yeah we  all make mistakes but you know just  think about it  you know that's what I've done I've  reflected a lot I've had a lot of time  that's one on one of the hardest things  about having a backpack is that you  spend a lot of time in your head if you  can't move right and that's what I've  done a lot of meditation a lot of  thinking a lot of analyzing and here we  are come full circle I feel like I came  full circle with him too I first  interviewed him in New York City about  14 years ago had done a lot of satellite  interviews but that was the first time  in person since that first one he's  always been very generous with me about  family and sharing some of those moments  and it just puts him in I think in a  light that a lot of us don't get to see  him so human he says he looks in the  mirror sometimes he's in his 40s he sees  the 20 year old sees the 20 year old but  knows a 20 year old gets further and  further away it's so interesting to hear  him talk about his kids the way they see  the game because they see the parents  suffering that what that work they go  through - just to be able to do it you  have to think about their lives in their  lives was most of the time that he was  injured until that masters when they  hadn't seen yeah his dominance and his I  guess the joy really that came from that  weekend and it was sort of a fluke that  they were there his his kids play soccer  his daughter had lost in a golf  tournament would have been playing on  Sunday it's like hey do you want to come  to Augusta National right sure why not  and he wasn't leading at the time he was  usually the guy that was leading on  Sunday and he'd win so it was really  fate in a way maybe and that had them  there you hear so much wisdom in his  voice in terms of just his own growth as  a man and his children have a lot to do  with that yeah and his appreciation of  the game yeah because of them and also  because it was almost taken away from  him in that way and to be able to play  like that I tell you when you're when  your kids look at you and and and they  they see your pain and your struggle and  they reflect it back to you you look at  everything differently yeah they really  do there is no doubt and interview thank  you and you can watch CBS Sports  coverage of the Northern Trust the first  playoff event of this season's FedEx Cup  starting Saturday right here on CBS and  you can catch more of my conversation  with Tiger Woods on CB